TI  - Adapting Video Foundation Models for Spatiotemporal Wildfire Forecasting via Cross-Modal Progressive Fine-Tuning
T2  - IEEE TRANSACTIONS ON GEOSCIENCE AND REMOTE SENSING
LA  - English
KW  - Foundation models
KW  - Geospatial analysis
KW  - Wildfires
KW  - Forecasting
KW  - Artificial intelligence
KW  - Adaptation models
KW  - Videos
KW  - Transformers
KW  - Data models
KW  - Spatiotemporal phenomena
KW  - Deep learning
KW  - Earth observation (EO)
KW  - foundation models
KW  - geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI)
KW  - progressive fine-tuning
KW  - satellite imagery
KW  - spatiotemporal forecasting
KW  - Vision Transformer (ViT)
KW  - wildfire spread prediction
AB  - Wildfires pose escalating threats to ecosystems, communities, and climate systems, highlighting the urgent need for accurate, high-resolution spatiotemporal forecasting. In this work, we explore the untapped potential of video foundation models for advancing wildfire spread prediction using multimodal satellite data. While large-scale foundation models have transformed artificial intelligence (AI) and show promise in geospatial AI (GeoAI), their direct application to domain-specific tasks like wildfire forecasting faces two major hurdles: 1) a substantial domain gap between general pretraining data (e.g., natural images and videos) and geospatial data (e.g., multispectral satellite imagery) and 2) limited labeled data for fine-tuning in real-world GeoAI tasks. To address these challenges, we introduce a cross-modal progressive fine-tuning (CMPF) strategy tailored for wildfire forecasting. CMPF combines: 1) Informed cross-modal architectural alignment, leveraging video-based Transformers pretrained on spatiotemporal tasks to better capture wildfire dynamics and 2) progressive fine-tuning, which gradually adapts models to wildfire-specific representations through intermediate domain adaptation before task-specific tuning. We evaluate CMPF using multiple Transformer backbones, including ViT, MViTv2, and VideoMAEv2, on wildfire spread forecasting benchmarks. Our results show that video foundation models, especially when fine-tuned progressively, outperform conventional convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and static vision Transformers in modeling wildfire evolution. These findings also validate the effectiveness of the proposed CMPF approach for adapting general-purpose AI foundation models to complex spatiotemporal geospatial tasks.
