TI  - Exposure-Aware commuting: Geo-AI-Driven route optimization to reduce NO2 in urban road networks
T2  - SUSTAINABLE CITIES AND SOCIETY
LA  - English
KW  - Route planning
KW  - Geo-AI
KW  - Nitrogen dioxide
KW  - Traffic-related pollutant
KW  - Exposure minimization
KW  - USE REGRESSION-MODEL
KW  - NITROGEN-DIOXIDE
KW  - POLLUTION
KW  - PM2.5
AB  - Air pollution exposure during daily commuting poses significant health risks, yet most route-planning studies focus on PM2.5 and rely on coarse spatial interpolation methods. This study advances exposure-aware mobility by developing a high-resolution geospatial artificial intelligence (Geo-AI) framework for Kaohsiung City, Taiwan. This framework models nitrogen dioxide (NO2), a traffic-related pollutant with sharp spatial gradients, and integrates it into an optimized route-planning system. A Light Gradient Boosting Machine Regression (LGBMR) model was trained on continuous monitoring data from December 2021 to February 2024. The model achieved robust predictive performance (R2 = 0.87), enabling reliable estimates of pollutant concentrations at the street segment scale. SHapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis revealed that chemical-industry density, wastetreatment facility density, and road network density were among the most influential predictors of elevated NO2. By combining 10 & times; 10 m NO2 concentration data from the model and a detailed road network database, we simulated 3000 randomized commuting routes and compared distance-optimized and exposure-optimized routing scenarios. On average, low-exposure routing reduced NO2 exposure by 2.04%, while 259 routes achieved reductions of over 5%. In some cases, the reduction reached 39.70%. A representative 365-day commuting simulation was conducted for the entire year of 2024. It projected an annual cumulative reduction in exposure of 9.02 & times; 105 ppb & sdot;s for a typical commuter using optimized routes. These results underscore the feasibility and public health potential of integrating high-resolution pollutant mapping with route optimization, enabling personalized, exposure-conscious mobility. It offers a scalable pathway for urban planners and policymakers to mitigate long-term health risks, especially in densely populated Asian cities with high motor vehicle use.
