KW  - Remote Sensing
KW  - Decision Support System
KW  - Semantic Segmentation
KW  - Microservice Architecture
KW  - Foundation Models
KW  - Mixture of Experts
AB  - The increasing volume of Earth Observation (EO) data presents a transformative opportunity for national development in sectors such as agriculture and urban planning. However, the operational complexity of traditional Geographic Information Systems (GIS) creates a significant bottleneck, restricting access to actionable insights for non-specialist decision-makers. This paper introduces the GeoAI-Platform, a modern, web-based system designed to bridge this gap by democratizing access to geospatial intelligence. We present a scalable microservice architecture grounded in principles of distributed systems and human-computer interaction. The architecture is organized into three distinct layers-Interaction, Orchestration, and Intelligence-which separate the user-facing interface, service coordination, and high-performance computation. The platform enables two core workflows: (1) on-demand AI analysis from user-defined Regions of Interest (ROIs), and (2) dynamic integration of user-provided imagery. As a proof of concept, the architecture was validated by fine-tuning two state-of-the-art foundation models (Prithvi and Satlas), which were combined using a Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) approach for cereal crop detection. The ensemble model achieved a Mean Intersection-over-Union (mIoU) of 95.74%, outperforming the individual models. The system is designed to be extensible, providing a clear pathway for integrating additional large-scale AI models for diverse geospatial tasks.
