TI  - An interpretable hybrid multiscale geographically weighted XGBoost (HMGWX) model with an empirical application to urban transport systems
T2  - COMPUTERS ENVIRONMENT AND URBAN SYSTEMS
LA  - English
KW  - Spatially explicit machine learning
KW  - Geospatial artificial intelligence (GeoAI)
KW  - Interpretability
KW  - Spatial heterogeneity
KW  - Non-linearity
AB  - Spatially explicit machine learning (SEML) models have recently been developed to address spatial heterogeneity and non-linearity. However, many existing SEMLs require post-hoc interpretability methods to extract the functional relationships between independent and dependent variables. Essentially, this type of SEMLs remain approximations derived from prediction outputs rather than from direct estimation of variable contributions, propagating prediction errors and potentially leading to biased interpretations of spatially varying processes. Additionally, SEMLs often face a trade-off between capturing non-linearity and spatial heterogeneity, meaning that a greater focus on non-linearity may reduce the capability of capturing spatial heterogeneity, and vice versa. These limitations constrain their ability to interpret geographical phenomenon. This study proposes a hybrid multiscale geographically weighted XGBoost (HMGWX), which integrates geographically weighted XGBoost (GWXGB) and geographically XGBoost (GXGB) within a semi-parametric additive model framework. The model assigns the most appropriate local estimator (GWXGB or GXGB) for each response-predictor relationship, balancing the ability to address non-linearity and spatial heterogeneity. HMGWX directly estimates the marginal contribution of each variable, thereby avoiding post-hoc errors propagated from prediction outputs. We validate HMGWX using both a synthetic dataset with known parameter surfaces and non-linear relationships, and a realworld ride-hailing waiting time dataset. Results show that HMGWX outperforms traditional spatially varying coefficient models (e.g., multiscale geographically weighted regression, Geographical Gaussian Process Generalized Additive Model) and existing SEMLs (e.g., XGBoost, Geographical-XGBoost, GWXGB, and GXGB) in both interpretability and predictive accuracy when spatial heterogeneity and non-linearity coexist. Compared with the baseline models, HMGWX reduces root mean square error by at least 8%. Furthermore, by introducing spatial clustering, early stopping, and dynamic bandwidth shrinking, we improve the computational efficiency of the calibration procedures for GWXGB, GXGB, and HMGWX, reducing total training time by over 76%. Finally, we propose an evaluation route map to guide appropriate model selection in practice.
