AB  - Geo Artificial Intelligence (GeoAI) advances education and access to knowledge, although it has sometimes exacerbated the digital divide between nations. UNESCO addresses this issue by promoting a comprehensive and sustainable approach to GeoAI as an educational tool, facilitating decision-making between administration and society, and promoting collaboration between educational actors. Our study proposal focuses on the Alc & aacute;zar Real, an eco-heritage asset in Ciudad Real (Castilla-La Mancha). The objective is to apply GeoAI to revalue heritage elements, giving them a new didactic and cultural functionality, and including students with functional diversity. The methodology includes an inventory of volcanic and heritage resources, using machine learning techniques and combining traditional methods of geographical science with TIG. Preliminary results suggest an ontology adapted to describe 3D models, promoting participation and educational innovation.
