TI  - Spatiotemporal Impacts of Vertical Urban Development Patterns on Carbon Emission Intensity: A GeoAI Framework Using Multisource Remote Sensing Data
T2  - REMOTE SENSING
LA  - English
KW  - vertical urban development
KW  - remote sensing
KW  - carbon emission intensity
KW  - GeoAI
KW  - interpretable machine learning
KW  - SHANGHAI
KW  - PROJECT
AB  - Highlights What are the main findings? A typology-aware GeoAI framework combining multisource geospatial data, MGWR, XGBoost, and SHAP showed that the relationship between vertical urban development and carbon emission intensity is spatially heterogeneous and time-sensitive across five major Chinese cities. Office-building POI density, population density, and functional diversity were consistently associated with CEI variation, while building height and the building-related greening proxy showed nonlinear and context-dependent association ranges rather than universal thresholds. What are the implications of the main findings? The carbon implications of vertical urban development cannot be interpreted using a single uniform rule; they depend on urban typology, local morphology, and development context. Multisource remote sensing and interpretable GeoAI can support context-sensitive low-carbon planning by identifying planning-relevant association ranges instead of fixed policy cutoffs.Highlights What are the main findings? A typology-aware GeoAI framework combining multisource geospatial data, MGWR, XGBoost, and SHAP showed that the relationship between vertical urban development and carbon emission intensity is spatially heterogeneous and time-sensitive across five major Chinese cities. Office-building POI density, population density, and functional diversity were consistently associated with CEI variation, while building height and the building-related greening proxy showed nonlinear and context-dependent association ranges rather than universal thresholds. What are the implications of the main findings? The carbon implications of vertical urban development cannot be interpreted using a single uniform rule; they depend on urban typology, local morphology, and development context. Multisource remote sensing and interpretable GeoAI can support context-sensitive low-carbon planning by identifying planning-relevant association ranges instead of fixed policy cutoffs.Abstract Urban carbon performance emerges from complex interactions among built form, functional mix, population concentration, and ecological conditions, making it difficult to interpret how vertical urban development shapes carbon emission intensity (CEI) across space and time. Existing approaches often rely on single-form indicators or context-insensitive models that overlook typological differentiation, spatial heterogeneity, and nonlinear effects. This study develops a typology-aware GeoAI framework to examine the association between vertical-development-oriented urban morphology and CEI across five major Chinese cities in 2005, 2010, 2015, and 2020 using multisource geospatial data, including ODIAC emissions, gridded GDP, building information, OSM-based POIs, WorldPop, and NDVI. Multiple machine-learning models were benchmarked; MGWR was used as a spatial explanatory reference, and SHAP was applied to recover nonlinear associations. XGBoost achieved the best cross-validation performance (R2 = 0.862, RMSE = 0.077, MAE = 0.004). Office-building POI density, population density, and functional diversity were consistently associated with CEI variation, whereas the dominant relationships were typology-specific and time-sensitive. Building height showed a nonlinear positive association in compact high-density cores, while the building-related greening proxy and office-building POI density exhibited recurring nonlinear patterns in mixed-use and expansion zones. Overall, the framework identifies planning-relevant association ranges rather than deterministic policy cutoffs.
