AB  - Accurate geolocalization is essential for emergency response and disaster management, enabling faster rescue efforts and reducing casualties. However, existing geolocalization methods often struggle with accuracy when processing images generated from social media and crowdsourcing platforms. This study significantly improves the geolocalization of volunteered geographic information (VGI) based on street view imagery (SVI) by integrating textual and visual information with the vision transformer large (ViT-Large) model. Using Hurricane Ian in 2022 as a case study, we demonstrate the effectiveness of our method at identifying geographic features and improving geolocation matching. Experiments conducted with standard traintest splits of 8:2 and 7:3 consistently outperformed the baseline methods. With an 8:2 data split, our method achieves a Recall@1 of 62.78%, while Recall@5, Recall@10, and Recall@1% reach 84.15%, 89.75%, and 90.37%, respectively. These improvements highlight the practicality of geolocalization for disaster response and provide valuable insights for future research.
