AB  - Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have demonstrated promising potential in addressing real-world vision-language tasks. However, existing MLLMs exhibit relatively limited capabilities for understanding street view images, struggling to achieve multilevel semantic understanding and spatial reasoning for region-level fine-grained inference, which, to some extent, limits their potential in geospatial artificial intelligence. This study constructs an instruction fine-tuning dataset, called SVGPT-Instruct, to guide MLLMs in learning spatial knowledge and scene features from street view images. This large-scale dataset for street view understanding comprises 430K instruction samples and 110K street view images. Ultimately, StreetViewGPT is proposed, an MLLM-based method for street view image scene perception and reasoning. It can uniformly handle a range of street view image tasks, including time estimation (TE), weather recognition (WR), scene classification (SC), spatial distance estimation (SDE), and scene graph generation (SGG). The experimental results show that in the TE, WR, and SC tasks, StreetViewGPT's accuracy improved by 1.33%, 21.68%, and 18.83%, respectively, compared to the suboptimal model. In the SDE task, the accuracy of predictions falling within the three error tolerance intervals of [75%, 125%], [50%, 150%], and [25%, 175%] reached 88.3%, 97.7%, and 99.5%, respectively, which improved by 78.2%, 78.7%, and 58.6%. In the SGG task, the Recall and Mean Recall reached 16.09% and 6.84%, respectively, which improved by 12.49% and 5.4% compared to Qwen2-VL. It demonstrates that StreetViewGPT possesses multilevel semantic understanding and spatial reasoning capabilities, ranging from global semantic perception at the image level to fine-grained reasoning at the region level. The proposed method enhances the recognition and cognition capabilities for spatial scenes in street view images, offering positive implications for smart city perception and autonomous driving navigation research.
