AB  - Web-based routing services have revolutionized accessibility research, but their inconsistent travel estimates for identical routes pose significant challenges to methodological reproducibility. This study develops a machine learning framework to predict pedestrian routing variability among Google Maps, ArcGIS, and OpenRouteService (ORS), transforming routing disagreement from a post-hoc observation into an anticipatable phenomenon. Using 10,000 residential-to-bus-stop routes in Athens-Clarke County, Georgia, Random Forest models were trained to predict where platforms will produce divergent travel time and distance estimates based on open-source predictors spanning network characteristics, topographic features, and demographic data. The models achieved reasonable predictive performance (distance model accuracy = 80.8%, time model accuracy = 82.1%) and revealed distinct feature importance patterns across models. For the distance model, network complexity metrics are the strongest predictors (40% combined importance), while topographic features dominate the time model (39% combined importance). The framework provides a first step toward targeting validation efforts where disagreements are probable, offering a potential path for reducing measurement uncertainty in pedestrian accessibility analyses.
