AB  - Indoor air quality (IAQ) critically influences children's health and development; however, predictive models tailored to child-specific household environments remain underdeveloped. This study applied a GeoAI-to-Indoor Environment model to predict PM1, PM2.5, and PM10 concentrations in bedrooms and living rooms of 86 children in Tainan, Taiwan, a high-pollution region. We recruited households during both warm and cold seasons (April 2021-December 2023), installed low-cost sensors at children's breathing height, and integrated building characteristics and activity patterns through household surveys. The proposed model achieved adjusted R-2 values exceeding 0.87 across particle size fractions and microenvironments. Bayesian Monte Carlo and SHapley Additive exPlanations sensitivity analyses identified outdoor PM2.5 as the most influential predictor (>50%), while human activities and building characteristics collectively explained 25-36% of indoor PM variation. Notably, PM level measured from the living room averaged 20% higher than bedrooms, driven primarily by proximity to kitchens and higher air exchange rates. This study advances the application of machine learning in environmental health research by providing a scalable and interpretable framework for indoor PM prediction. It offers a robust scientific foundation for air pollution control strategies, strengthens exposure assessment in epidemiological studies, and delivers evidence-based recommendations for providing actionable evidence for IAQ interventions and improving IAQ in child-specific households within highly-polluted areas.
